    description: foreground pixels lie on 1-2 down-right wrapping diagonals (d = (c-r) mod N)
    count: 1-2 distinct diagonal indices
    coverage_per_diagonal: 50%-100% of N pixels populated
    segment_contiguity: each diagonal's populated rows form a single contiguous range (no gaps)
    wrapping: a diagonal may wrap around (row hits N, col wraps to 0 or vice versa), creating 1-2 non-wrapping segments from a single contiguous wrapping segment
  runs:
    definition: maximal non-wrapping down-right segments of foreground pixels
    count: 1-3 total (one wrapping diagonal with coverage>N produces 2 runs)
    length: 3-11 per run
  constraint: no two foreground pixels are orthogonally adjacent (4-connected)
  transform:
    for each run, index pixels 0,1,2,...; recolor odd-indexed pixels to yellow (4)
    alternation restarts at each run's top-left endpoint

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (constraints are easily satisfied constructively)
  steps:
    1. Pick N in 3-12 and a foreground color from {1,2,3,5,6,7,8,9} (not yellow=4)
    2. Pick 1-2 distinct diagonal indices d in 0..N-1
    3. For each diagonal d, pick a contiguous range of rows [start, start+len) mod N with len in ceil(N/2)..N
    4. Place foreground at (r, (d+r) mod N) for each r in the contiguous range
    5. Verify no two foreground pixels are orthogonally adjacent; if violated, retry step 2-3
    6. The no-adjacency constraint is almost always satisfied: two diags d1,d2 conflict only when their column separation at a shared row is exactly 1, which is rare and easily rejected
